  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 105 AM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThursday - Friday: For the later part of the week, the wet pattern \ncontinues for south central and southeastern Colorado. Moist \nsouthwesterly flow, with additional embedded shortwaves, will be \nstreaming over the region. This pattern will bring additional rounds \nof showers and thunderstorms to the area. The greatest coverage of \nprecipitation will be on Thursday, with another day of scattered to \nwidespread showers and storms. Coverage is expected to lessen some \nthrough Friday as the deeper moisture lessens and is pushed east, \nwith coverage more isolated to scattered in nature. Along with that, \nprecipitation Friday will favor the mountains more, as weaker \nbroader ascent becomes reinforced by orographic forcing. Beyond all \nof that, another couple of cloudy and breezy days is anticipated, \nthough with cloud cover starting to thin out Friday. Also like \nWednesday, the breeziest winds are expected across the eastern \nplains. Looking at temperatures, a slow warming trend begins, with \nhigh temperatures Thursday remaining slightly below seasonal, and \nthen warming to near seasonal Friday. Then for lows, they follow \ntheir respective trend, with lows Thursday night above seasonal \nlows, and then cooling back to around seasonal lows Friday night. \n\nSaturday - Sunday: For the weekend, quieter weather makes a brief \nreturn to south central and southeastern Colorado. Ridging will \nstart to develop over the area, bringing greater subsidence and \ndrier area. With this pattern in place, dry conditions are expected \nto prevail for much of the area. With that said though, a rogue \nshower can not be ruled out along the mountains given weak forcing \nand modest moisture in place. Otherwise, mostly clear to partly \ncloud skies with fairly light winds is anticipated. Temperatures \nwill also continue the warming trend, with much of the region \nwarming back to above seasonal values. \n\nMonday - Tuesday: Now heading into early next week, we return to an \nactive pattern. Troughing is expected to push across south central \nand southeastern Colorado during this timeframe. Confidence is \nmedium to high (50-70%) in this feature passing over the area given \nstrong agreement between ensemble guidance. However, there is still \nsome uncertainty in how this pattern ultimately evolves, such as how \namplified the troughing becomes and how much moisture is pulled \nnorthward ahead of it. Regardless, this pattern is expected to bring \nincreased precipitation chances to the area Monday into early \nTuesday. The greatest coverage of precipitation is anticipated to \nremain along the mountains, where forcing will be best. Then through \nTuesday, drier conditions start to return on the backside of the \ntroughing. Outside of precipitation concerns, clouds and winds are \nexpected to increase with this pattern. Then looking at \ntemperatures, Monday would be expected to remain above seasonal \nvalues ahead of the system, with temperatures falling Tuesday behind \nit and with a potential cold front passage.",
